..

  I've been chatting with the C RTL engineer and with one of the local
  OpenVMS memory management gurus.  I'm completely baffled about the 
  abovementioned solution -- neither the C RTL nor the global section 
  system services handle access to the shared memory from multiple nodes 
  within a cluster.  Across accessors on the same node, yes... 

  My recollection is that shared memory is node-local.  Further, the 
  backing storage accesses "behind" shared memory section updates are 
  not coordinated across cluster nodes, hence updates can be lost...

  Unless there's something really nifty happening deep inside the XQP 
  or the I/O subsystem here that none of us know about or remember, I
  don't see how this approach would be reliable...

  If you handle your own intra-cluster communications, you could work
  out a scheme where the information was distributed to the shared
  memory of other cluster members.  (Or of course -- if this was an 
  OpenVMS Galaxy configuration -- you could use galactic memory...)
  If the above discussion intends to describe the creation of your
  own intra-cluster protocol (based on the lock manager) to coordinate 
  the updates, then that might be feasible...  (I'd want to watch for 
  the potential of some interesting timing issues on the completions 
  of the updates to the backing section.  And I'd take a serious look 
  at just passing over the information, via an application network 
  protocol connection or via RTR or otherwise -- the "fun" part comes 
  in because there isn't really any direct control over when OpenVMS
  decides to write pages of the memory information out to the backing 
  storage, and this I/O can occur in the absence of an $updsec call.)

  We do need to update the msync documentation as well as the various
  shared memory documentation -- I've passed along that report to the
  technical writers for inclusion into the next release of the OpenVMS
  programming concepts and system service reference manuals...

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
